title: 'Complementary learning systems: the new is learned fast apart, then folded slowly into the old by replaying both together; learned fast and alone, it overwrites the old.'
pid: 5222eae7-a814-47ae-b5f8-6889229b1b25
body: |2-
  McClelland, McNaughton and O'Reilly, 1995. The hippocampus keeps
  episodes quickly; the cortex learns slowly, from replays, often in
  sleep, interleaved with what it already holds. Training a network on
  the new alone makes it lose the old ("catastrophic interference").
  The team's mapping: a mission is the episode, a graph the cortex,
  tending and dreaming the replay. It agrees with what Gen found
  published about minds going back over their memory:
  [[id:797628ed-c9fb-4a08-8922-d59eb854eed5][Going back over a graph with no task in hand helps when it moves, links and checks claims against their sources, and harms when it rewrites.]]
